# Coral Bhasma
Tamil title: பவழபஸ்பம்

"Mistress of every art! The serpent couch upon which you recline was bought with the profit I made selling sandalwood logs to Lord Chandrasekara. Do you know where that full-length mirror in the marble room, before which you preen every evening, came from? People were saying I had brought Elappan great misery. To calm his anxiety, I cleverly used my intelligence and said I had heard he bought it for sixty gold pieces, though I knew he had paid six hundred. He surrendered it in tears; now you stand smiling before it in all your finery.

"Vedanta did not find you your clothes, pleasures, mansion, garden, carriage, vehicles, and servants. They are no products of magic. They say you and your companions declared in the People's Forum that the arrogance of those who make plunderous profits must be crushed. Yet you live amid the goods produced by that very plunder. You accused us of drinking the blood of the poor. I removed its foul smell, poured it into a golden cup, and gave it to you; you drank it too.

"I did all this for you. I obtained a station worthy of your beauty and made you a spectacle admired by everyone. Whenever you ate milk and fruit in perfect contentment and slept upon a fragrant mattress, I drowned myself in accursed accounts to provide you all these pleasures. Now you speak at the People's Forum! I picked you from among the common crowd and made you a great lady, yet you stood in that forum hissing at men like me.

"Senseless woman! I am a merchant. Profit is my profession, and you enjoy its fruit. Forgetting both your comforts and the gratitude due to the man who lies with his account books to provide them, you listened to somebody's words and spoke wildly. Is that right? Had I not conducted my trade capably, where would you be? What would you be doing? Poor creature, in the blazing midday sun you would carry a rice pot upon your head to the market shed and serve me as a maid. I have kept you like a crowned queen. And now, under the king's orders, you propose to torment and destroy cunning and crown compassion!"

Her husband spat these words in fury. Without even wishing to wipe away her tears, he made his wife listen in distress. She had thought he spoke so sharply only from anger. Only after he said it did the truth begin to strike her; that was precisely why she suffered so greatly.

It was a time when the golden sayings of the Buddha were spreading through the land. Some rulers had embraced Buddhism. Even in halls established to preserve the ancient faith, some adopted the new path and became bhikkhus. Prudent kings understood that trying to halt the rising Buddhist faith would be like paying to purchase a powerful enemy. They built a temple to Rudradeva and a Buddhist monastery beside it.

The priestly order of the orthodox faith grew angry whenever it saw Buddhist monks, whom it regarded as innovators undermining its authority. The priests did not show this openly. They praised Buddhism as a home of great Aryan truths and a birthplace of purity, and declared its noble doctrines extraordinary. Having lulled the innovators with praise, they slowly tied Buddhism to their own faith: all these principles, they said, already existed in the Upanishads and formed the essence of the Vedas. Their deceit moved like a serpent twisting through green grass. It could not leap like a tiger, because many kings supported the new path and orthodoxy could survive only by cunning.

A great change was taking place among the people: weariness, and even distaste, towards luxury. Hearing monks narrate the Buddha's life, their hearts melted. Hunting endlessly for pleasure came to seem a chase after a mirage. The thought that luxury was futile brought another question: why should a few roll in pleasure while the many suffer? Was it not cruel to keep people in such a condition? Why should a few perpetrate this cruelty, accumulate plundered wealth, and live in turreted mansions amid gardens with tanks? Carrying pearls, coral, diamonds, cat's-eyes, and silk between countries for trade; selling domestic goods for ten times their value; loading unbearable burdens upon the poor; reducing labourers to beggars while a small group lived with palanquins and retinues - were these not injustices?

Such thoughts produced an association called the People's Forum. Born beneath a tree, it moved to a public shelter and finally held its meetings in a handsome upper-storeyed house.

Maruthavalli was the beautiful daughter of the poet Agaval Ambalavanan and wife of a merchant. Ambalavanan's poems spoke of the suffering of the poor, so the seeds of the Forum's ideas were already planted in her heart. As its members spoke, those seeds sprouted and grew into vines. Through her effort and assistance, the Forum began meeting in that beautiful house. Maruthavalli moved from listening to others to addressing the Forum herself. Drawing rare insights from her father's fine poetry, she gave its members a new vision. Some Buddhist monks also began to help them.

The king was shrewd in ruling people. "Build a monastery for the shaven-headed and a temple for those who tie up their hair, and law and peace will endure. What is there to fear?" Such was his system. He began to hear of the Forum precisely when Maruthavalli's fame was rising. At first he worried. Then he thought, "Oh, the Maruthavalli who gives life to the Forum is the wife of our Navakodiar. There is nothing to fear. A small stratagem can weaken it."

When the merchant Navakodiar came as usual, hands folded and mouth closed, to ask whether the king had any business for him, the king offered him a seat and ordered a servant to bring a cool drink.

Smiling, the king said, "Navakodiar, we grant you the right to cut and sell timber for two years in the sandalwood forest on our frontier. The charter is ready."

Navakodiar knelt and expressed his gratitude.

"Danger may arise while cutting timber in the forest. I shall also issue an order allowing you to employ ten soldiers," the king added.

The merchant could not discern the reason for such royal kindness. He knew only that a king's affection sometimes warned of danger. He began to ponder.

"Navakodiar, I wish to tell you a secret. By God's ordinance, king and mendicant and everyone else has been assigned a station. To call this philosophy false is sinful. Certainly one must help the poor - why else does the Compassionate One grant wealth? We must establish shelters, eating houses, and rest houses. But may anyone say that the one who created poverty is not God? May anyone claim that a poor man's condition arises not from fate but from the conspiracies of lords? Those who speak such sinful words will not merely fall into the deepest abyss; they will lead the people into inescapable hell."

"Your Majesty, what doubt is there?" Navakodiar replied with his usual lesson.

"Punishing those who commit such improper acts would take no time," said the king.

"Would not one word suffice? The soldiers would drag that babbling, world-corrupting rabble straight to the gallows," the merchant said heroically.

"True. But if we issued such an order, would not your noble wife Maruthavalli also have to die?"

The king had loosed his final arrow. The merchant fell at his feet, rose, and pleaded. The king then told him of Maruthavalli's speeches and warned him to rebuke her immediately and make her sever every connection with the Forum.

Only then did Navakodiar condemn Maruthavalli.

She understood that it was impossible to argue with and reform a husband who had lost his character in the search for money. She saw something else even more clearly: she could not embrace the thought, "Let us avoid displeasing my husband. Are we alone responsible for removing the country's barbarous ways? Let others attend to it; we shall remain the lamp of the household." Her heart had grown too strong to forget principle for the sake of life. Courage arose to work henceforth for that principle and, for its sake, to obtain "release."

There had been a time when her beautiful face and loving eyes transported Navakodiar - before his chests filled with pearls, coral, and cat's-eyes. At first he had worried, "We enjoy influence at court, yet instead of mingling happily with princesses, this Marutham clings to useless things called virtue, justice, love, grace, truth, and the righteous path." After the king's warning, dislike of her grew into hatred. His passion for Vennila intensified it.

Vennila was a palace maid whose ornaments concealed her lack of beauty and whose song and dance concealed her deceit. Maruthavalli understood clearly that she could no longer remain Navakodiar's wife. Midnight meetings and hurried embraces no longer satisfied him; the fire of desire had grown until Vennila had to live in his mansion. A palm-leaf message was therefore sent to the poet: advise your daughter to behave properly, or take her away.

That was one of Vennila's victories, cheaply obtained with only a few sweet words.

The future of the People's Forum depended upon Maruthavalli's position. She had spoken sparks, invoked virtue and struggle, and lacked no slogans. If, on learning that she would lose her comfortable mansion, she tied up her principles and threw them into the loft to resume the life of a fine lady, the Forum would be plunged into confusion and soon close. Maruthavalli knew it well. Her inner battle was fierce because the Forum's future was joined to her decision.

She had no base desire to betray anyone. Yet she worried that people, unaware she left her husband for principle, might suspect immoral conduct. She also feared Vennila would spread empty rumours. Her pillow grew wet and her hair lost its shine. At last she passed beyond anxiety and fear and told the poet everything in her heart without concealment.

"I am proud, daughter, proud! You seem virtue and courage made flesh. Come, child. We shall go home," he said. A smile long absent returned to her face.

"Let her disappear. If she wants money as compensation for the defect of having been my wife, I shall pay," Navakodiar said.

"Money? No, son. That is the only thing you possess; bind it up and guard it," the old man replied.

Maruthavalli boldly stopped Navakodiar when he moved to attack. Father and daughter left the mansion. The king rejoiced; Vennila held a feast for her friends.

The poet felt some weariness at the thought that ignorant people might speak degradingly of his daughter. Yet he trusted that through a pure life enduring slander and excellence in service, Maruthavalli could defeat their ridicule. He planned to return to the village, establish a school for children, and have her work there.

Maruthavalli had another plan. At the edge of town she touched her father's feet and said, "Father, you may now go home. Here is where I must go," pointing to a monastery established to teach the Buddhist path.

For a moment he was stunned. Then he understood that her decision could not be changed and that this sacred refuge would place her beyond the touch of public ridicule and opposition.

"Daughter, who am I to forbid you? I am old and hoped for your support, but a pure light calls you. Go, child, and serve. Until my final breath, I too shall continue the work of enlightenment."

Maruthavalli entered the monastery; the poet went to his village; Vennila entered Navakodiar's mansion.

The People's Forum rejoiced. Maruthavalli's "sacrifice" made its greatness known throughout the region. Hope became visible that the capacity to surrender life's pleasures for principle would grow and Buddhism would triumph.

King Marthandan's rage could no longer be restrained. He decided to strike decisively. Orders went forth to dissolve Buddhist monasteries and imprison conspirators. The place where Maruthavalli stayed was not strictly a monastery but an institution that taught the moral excellence of Buddhism and conducted a campaign to cleanse social corruption. Citing its links to the Forum, Marthandan accused it of plotting, beneath the screen of the new religion, to invite a foreign king to invade the country.

Law began to rain blows. Maruthavalli rejoiced. The Forum's teaching of virtue had frightened an authoritarian king. A ruler who had strutted arrogantly asking what these people could accomplish had already reached the point of believing that only their imprisonment could preserve his authority. The speed of that change filled her with pride and delight.

When she learned her companions were prepared not merely for prison but torture, her pride rose higher. "Victory! Victory!" she said within herself. The king's soldiers screamed, "Seize them! Beat them!" In Maruthavalli's ears it sounded only as "Victory! Victory!"

"Tell the truth. What is the conspiracy?"

"Ask what our plan is; there is no conspiracy. Our plan is to give knowledge and freedom to people trapped between an arrogant king and a deceitful priest."

"A war of liberation, heroic woman? Will your eyes become spears and your words arrows? 'Forum,' indeed! A cave in which worthless people gather to destroy country and religion, named a forum!"

"Who here are worthless? We who dare surrender life for a noble principle, ignore the roaring idlers who grow fat in service to the ruler, and labour for the people's welfare and to remove corruption from religion - are we the worthless crowd?"

The chief interrogator could speak no further. Fire flew from Maruthavalli's words. Whips fell until blood burst from her body, but she did not tremble. Seeing her fearless heart, the others shed what little fear remained. They swore that even for release they would not abandon their campaign.

"Where did these creatures find such courage? Does a Buddha who wandered somewhere have armies throughout the country? What wonder is this?" the prison chief thought. Buddhism nearly embraced even him; with great effort he held it back.

Marthandan, proclaiming that the righteous kingdom would crush revolution and give no entrance to the new faith, did not stop with imprisoning preachers like Maruthavalli. He made the occasion a source of revenue. Along with imprisoning revolutionaries and advocates of the new path, he confiscated their property. Many wealthy people wholly unconnected with it were jailed on suspicion and forced to pay enormous fines. Fertile fields, splendid mansions, and heaps of gold were seized.

New terror spread among the rich. To show they had no connection to the new faith, many conducted chariot festivals, processions, ablutions, and worship for the old gods and displayed the guise of devotees.

The festival procession Vennila conducted for the Veludaiyan temple churned Navakodiar's stomach because of its tremendous expense, but won her garlands of praise from town dignitaries and high families. Even the palace discussed the radiance of her "devotion." Vennila rejoiced that she had removed the danger facing Navakodiar - until news came that his former wife Maruthavalli was to accuse him. Then Vennila sobbed.

Dhanappan, chief of the special religious tribunal, had once been a flower-stringer at the temple. Vennila had rejected his advances then. Now, as she approached to secure Navakodiar's safety, his thoughts rushed between resentment and desire:

"Did you not show me a scowling face, deceitful woman? You mocked me, 'Behave according to your saffron robe.' I called your eyes lotuses, your lips coral, your teeth pearls, and you sweet honey. You said such conduct did not suit a mendicant. You despised me because I merely strung flowers for the temple. Today I am chief of the religious tribunal, and now you fall at my feet!

"Do not imagine I will fall to your enchanting speech. You grew swollen-headed when you found Navakodi. Suffer now! His property will be confiscated and you will be imprisoned with him. You still dare smile. I shall not become a victim of that trick. Your seductive look cannot deceive me. Stand farther away, Vennila. Loosening your garment will not overturn my resolve. Direct that murderous gaze at some other fool. Go! Why stand here? Do not come near me. Get up; why sit beside me? You are pure, wifely gold, are you not? How may you touch me? I want none of your play. Do not extinguish the lamp. Let go! Away, bewitching thief! Who wants your kiss? No, impossible, no... do not push me down... Vennila... beloved!

"Where had you hidden all this desire? How long I waited for this pleasure! You give the nectar of the gods. Do not speak; you must not speak. I will never release you. Am I not a follower of Siva, who gave half his body to Uma? Sakti - our Sakti! Ah, Navakodi enjoyed this feast. Dear Vennila, command me. What must I do? Save him? I shall do it for you. Once it is done, will you forget me? Will this love endure? You will come often, will you not, my love, my delight?"

Dhanappan showered hymns in the upper bedchamber of the tribunal; Vennila supplied a corresponding feast of expression and gesture. A plan to save Navakodiar was formed, and light filled both the room and her mind.

While she pleased him, Dhanappan imagined that for Vennila he might free not merely Navakodiar but every prisoner. By sunrise the thought had dried. Vennila, her mind full of light, had broken his rudraksha rosary the previous night - could he forget what she had told him?

Following Vennila's arrangement, Dhanappan held a conversation with another official. They spoke of an alleged conspiracy to introduce Buddhism, the king's fury, and the need to show no mercy. Navakodiar entered and fell at their feet.

"Do not panic, man," Dhanappan said. "What can be done? Your evil deed has assumed her form."

"Somehow she must be rescued - not for her sake. If people learn my wife is such a woman, my honour will go and my business be ruined."

"True. Your property may also be confiscated."

"Yes, the king is enraged. Would he not be angry at a plot to overthrow the State?"

"The king is not angry merely from fear of losing his crown. It is religion - our ancient religion and Vedic way - that this group seeks to destroy."

"Last night, while Vennila shone, I was thinking of this very matter. Navakodiar, why did you take a conspiratorial woman as your companion? Did her beauty blind you? However beautiful she may be, how could you desire one who destroys religion?"

"You alone must show me the good path."

"No one seeing her could call her a conspirator, sir."

"Let us grant that her face is the moon and that she deserves praise as a golden or flowering vine. For a woman's love, should one lose religion?"

"I agreed to lose her. It is our former connection that has now become a danger."

"I am thinking whether anything can be done. None of this danger would have arisen had people like you behaved properly. When we ask for funds to build a monastery or conduct Maheswara worship, you open an empty hand."

"I am ready to make a donation. It is enough if Maruthavalli is declared innocent."

"Her name is Maruthavalli? A beautiful name. Merchant, what is your age - not sixty?"

"I have completed fifty. Anxiety makes me look old."

"And this Marutham is said to be twenty?"

"Nearly thirty."

"Very well. I shall declare Marutham mentally unsound and obtain a doctor's statement. She will be released. I trust I need not explain further. Donate a small village for the worship of the bilva leaf."

"I shall obey your command."

Vennila's feast and the rich man's donation bore fruit. Carrying Dhanappan's order and the physician's palm leaf, Navakodiar went to the prison authority.

"Is that so? I suspected it myself," the man said, and ordered her release.

Maruthavalli laughed and laughed.

"Mad? Me? Did my husband say so? It is true, sir, perfectly true. Many times he called me mad: 'What do we lack? We have a lovely mansion and abundant wealth. Instead of enjoying them happily, you put the town's troubles upon your head and suffer. What madness is this?' Yes, sir, I am mad. In the sea that grants delight I see a pleasure boat, but upon it money-seekers with ruined vision dance. Naturally I become confused and afraid."

The prison chief pitied her, believing the madness had advanced. Navakodi took her to the mansion. Vennila's eyes asked a thousand questions; she did not understand Maruthavalli's laughter.

"Your name is Vennila? A lovely name. He is very fond of you? Of course. Your eyes awaken desire. Vennila, I could not become the woman suited to him. May you remove that deficiency," Maruthavalli said.

Vennila became slightly afraid. They had cleverly caused her to be called mad to check the king's anger, but perhaps she truly was insane.

They could not keep Maruthavalli confined in the mansion for long. Another fear entered Vennila: what if Navakodi began to desire his wife again? She planned to drive Maruthavalli away. Meanwhile the poet arrived after hearing the news, and father and daughter once more left the mansion.

"Your body has withered, daughter."

"My mind has grown fertile, Father."

"You have grown dark, my dear."

"Only the body. There is new light within me now."

They spoke without anxiety, unconcerned that they had lost mansion and luxurious life.

Fools mocked, "When is the revolution coming? Will it arrive at least this month of Aadi?"

"Look at the old man's staff. It is a magic wand!" an idler jeered.

Maruthavalli laughed. "Father, we must cure all these people too. There is much work; we must labour without rest."

And so they did, helping the destitute. They occupied a ruined temple and sheltered there people who were hunchbacked, blind, deaf, mute, or missing limbs. They begged and gathered small donations to sustain them.

The State, which had never shown the slightest concern for these sufferers, moved quickly on learning they had made a temple their home - not to save the people, but to protect the temple.

"Hey, old man! Have you turned the Dandayudhapani temple into a monastery for free eaters?" the king's soldiers demanded. Maruthavalli answered for her father.

"The temple was ruined. Nobody comes here now."

"And so you made it a house for these creatures?"

"Only bats had been circling here."

"We know you are talkative. Do you know the punishment for polluting a temple?"

"I did not pollute it. I swept out and removed the rubbish that lay here."

"Do not chatter. We return tomorrow morning. The place must be empty, or you will count prison bars again."

They left to report to their superior. Maruthavalli sighed, looked at the terrified destitute, and said, "Embodiments of compassion refuse to witness this ugly sight."

"Where would they find the time, daughter? Chariots and festivals consume it all. When can they look at people without arms and legs? Come, let us take them elsewhere," the old poet said.

"Where else can we go, Father?"

His eyes full of light, he replied, "Where did the Blessed Buddha go, despite possessing a beautiful palace? Through forests, hills, and everywhere. We are food-bowl mendicants; what does the place matter? Come, daughter. Every tree's shade is our mansion."

Maruthavalli raised her father's hands to her eyes. "My teacher, Father. You are my teacher of wisdom."

The shade of a great tree became their new mansion, and their service continued.

Vennila too had to continue her "service": Dhanappan refused to release her. At first she was angry that he exploited the situation to torment her. Later it became familiar and began to yield benefits. Navakodi grew suspicious but lacked the courage to ask. By the time he found it, Vennila had become a tigress.

"Everything is only to prevent danger to you," she told Navakodi. "I cannot bear that old man's harassment," she told Dhanappan. She knew Dhanappan had become a rich man.

Dhanappan repeatedly threatened Navakodi, who was rapidly becoming an ordinary man. Vennila moved into a new mansion Dhanappan provided. Navakodi felt grief and anger, but what could he do?

Dhanappan threw a new bomb: "The king often asks about you. I have tried many times to placate him. Somehow Marutham must be brought back to the ancient faith. Until you accomplish it, suspicion will remain upon you and danger may come at any time. There is no use blaming me."

"He stole Vennila, my wealth has melted, and now he brutally threatens me. What can I do?" Navakodi grieved.

Dhanappan had a reason for his new course. Agents of the ancient faith enticed and frightened converts, praised orthodoxy, and denounced Buddhism as mere atheism. They successfully circulated false testimonies:

"I heard seductive speech and was ruined."

"Her eyes were wine jars; I was deceived and became an atheist."

"My skull itched, my limbs convulsed, and my sight went out. God came in a dream and ordered me to return. I recognized my error and abandoned the new path; instantly the evil departed and I was blessed."

Such propaganda renewed attachment to the ancient faith among people without clarity.

Dhanappan wanted the reward and acclaim for a great victory: Maruthavalli's abandonment of Buddhism and return to orthodoxy. He became a scorpion. Navakodi writhed.

The monastery beneath the tree expanded. Responsibility and burden grew. Father and daughter laboured without tiring, assisted by many companions. Aid arrived now and then from regions where Buddhism had spread, but it became a mustard seed beside the growing number of refugees fleeing orthodox persecution elsewhere.

As their numbers rose, severe disease invaded. The physician's efforts brought inadequate results: cough, consumption, fever, congested phlegm, convulsions, and many other illnesses. If coral bhasma could be prepared, the dangerous disease might be checked, the physician said. High-grade coral was costly; Maruthavalli was surrounded only by destitute mendicants. He supplied whatever herbs were available, sighed, and repeated that coral bhasma would help.

Maruthavalli grieved as conditions worsened. At the same time she learned of Navakodiar's plight under Dhanappan and pitied him.

"Dhanappan has become a cobra with raised hood. He says you must return to the ancient faith or he will take revenge upon me. What can I do, Maruthavalli? I have lost enormous sums feeding his greed and cannot continue forever. The duty to save me is yours. As long as you remain in the new faith, I am in danger," Navakodi lamented.

"Shall we leave for another country?" she suggested.

His only answer was, "What will become of my lands, gardens, groves, and business here?"

Should she find a way to obtain coral bhasma, or a way to prevent this pitiable man's danger? A fierce war arose in her mind. Dhanappan fixed a deadline. Little remained except for Navakodi to fall at Maruthavalli's feet.

Maruthavalli returned to the mansion. Those under the tree were shocked; even Dhanappan was astonished; Navakodi danced with joy. The town began saying she had abandoned the new faith and rejoined the old. Some questioned her directly. She smiled and gave no other answer.

The poet shed tears and had no heart to visit his daughter. "Foolish girl! That was all she could manage," he muttered.

Though the king used harsh means to crush the new path, its followers willingly endured cruelty. The priestly order, which had made the ancient faith a means of livelihood and authority, was alarmed. It tried with renewed enthusiasm to spread orthodoxy through delightful songs, tasty stories, and entertaining dances. The result fell short of its desire.

Those subjected to cruelty remembered how much the Buddha had suffered to discover truth and gained unprecedented courage. Reports came from many directions that arrogant kings and haughty lords had become virtuous under Buddhism's golden light; that people immersed in pleasure had seen its new radiance, removed desire, and renounced selfishness.

The oppressed people of the righteous kingdom believed that if not today, then tomorrow, the Buddha's light would spread there too. Those who boiled at the cruelties yearned to destroy the unjust rule.

On one side, a Buddhist storm changing people's minds. On the other, the spark of revolution demanding the destruction of tyranny. What if the two great forces united? This was King Marthandan's fear.

Despite royal favour, the priestly order grew angry and sorrowful as popular opposition increased. It laboured without rest to make the people devotees of both king and ancient faith. Yet even some who retained attachment to orthodoxy showed eagerness to remove the evils in the king's administration. Some supporters of monarchy argued that popular resistance arose from the fraud of priests who used religion for selfish ends.

The priests found it difficult to debate them.

"The king is an aspect of God. One becomes a king through merit earned in a former birth."

"What if the king commits injustice? Is that not ruinous to country and people?"

"Siva, Siva! Who will oppose the king - the people? Will the people change the system of rule? What right have they, and how could they do it? To destroy an aspect of God is to insult God. Will He remain silent?"

"People are dying. Their property is plundered and freedom crushed."

"Sorrowful events, certainly."

"Women are violated; men are bound and burned alive."

"The heart must tremble."

"A people rising in fury can gain strength to oppose and destroy any army. They can reduce prisons to dust, turn ornamental audience halls into mounds of earth, and burn the wicked man's palace."

"They cannot, and must not even think they can. The thought itself is a great sin."

"Sin? Is setting out to save people a great sin?"

"You lack intelligence. Would you perform the work of the God who created and protects you? The sun burns only at His command. It could reduce the world to ash, but does it? Does God permit it? In a blink the sun could drink the ocean and turn its waves into a sand-blown desert. Does it? God will not allow it. The Almighty knows what must be done, how, and when. He alone knows."

"Who doubted God's power? We say the king's injustice must be opposed and destroyed. Unless it is removed, the people cannot be freed from suffering."

"God knows how and when to relieve suffering. Do you make rain or wind? Madman! God alone can reform the king and change government. Mahesan uses many ways to test people. He may make the king impose more taxes, cast people and even leaders into prison, and when their suffering becomes unbearable, amuse himself by observing their condition and thoughts."

"People now understand clearly that the king, not Mahesan, commits these cruelties. That is why resistance rises among them."

"It rises first and falls later. Do you know? Maruthavalli, who said she wanted neither mansion nor husband and wandered with the new religionists, is about to return to our ancient faith. Truth will triumph; one needs patience."

The priests lamented among themselves. They had called the innovators revilers of the Vedas, outcastes, founders of heretical faiths, and enemies of observance. They had warned them of total destruction and God's wrath. They had tried to stop a flood with haystacks, and the result was zero.

"Why does fear no longer arise in people's minds? Those who once trembled, believing they would enter Raurava hell if they crossed the line we drew, now stand upright. If we shout too loudly, they knit their brows. How did things change so? Formerly, when we narrated the divine sports of Sriman Narayana, they melted. When Gopala Krishna put the flute to his lips and poured out sweet music, the tiger about to kill, the dying cow, the hunter raising his bow, the bird about to die, and all living beings attained divine bliss. How the people rejoiced and worshipped Govinda's name!"

"When we described Krishna singing and dancing with the gopis in Brindavan, every face became a red lotus."

"Offerings piled up and worship was splendid. We enjoyed a station worthy of the name 'gods upon earth.' That golden age is gone."

"Ask for a ritual fee and they call it waste. Ask for a sacrificial cow and they call it violence. Ask for a donation and they ask whether it is official begging. Call ourselves ascetics and they demand to know what we renounced. Their conduct is unbearable, and we cannot understand or reverse it."

"It is all the result of the storm Buddha raised."

"Can a Buddhist storm destroy the faith of the Lord who created all worlds?"

"Who is Buddha but a creation of the Lord? Some even call him the Lord himself."

One priest offered a reason for Buddhism's spread: the Buddha's story melted the people. A prince renounced royal pleasure, beautiful wife, and child; wandered forests; and served humanity. Siddhartha's suffering to discover the cause of sorrow and the road to release dissolved listeners' hearts. They were entranced by accounts of his golden body fading and growing thin as a twig while he wandered without food, thought deeply, and found wisdom.

Another protested: "He renounced royal pleasure and wandered forests? Rama spent fourteen years in the wilderness, wore bark, ate fruit, and slept upon the earth. The story of Rama melts even stone."

"It did formerly. Now people say Sri Rama went to the forest and lived there fourteen years only to preserve his father's word, not for the people, whereas Buddha renounced royalty for humanity."

"They ask because no warrior exists now to cut out their tongues!"

"After suffering in the forest, Rama returned and enjoyed his coronation. Buddha became and remained a renunciant. That difference too captivates the people."

"We shall see how long. The new path spreads region by region. It advances here because no proper sacrifice has been held for years. There are temple rites, but what other special observance? The king delays, worried about expense. He burns money for dancers but opens an empty hand for God's work."

They discussed continuing Bhagavata stories carefully and with concealment. The story of Mahavishnu requesting three feet of land from Mahabali, expanding to cosmic form, measuring earth and heaven in two strides, and placing the third upon Mahabali's head now invited dangerous questions. They must not lose patience. The Buddhist storm was great, but they needed confidence in their strength to manage it.

"Look at the wonder," one said. "Did not Maruthavalli, who abandoned mansion life and dared prison, agree to become a fine lady again? Observe Navakodiar's skilful victory. We must turn her change of heart into a magnificent sacred tale for the people."

"It is lightning in black darkness. We must not let it appear and vanish; we must make it a divine radiance. Say Maruthavalli was deceived by the monks' lies, opposed her husband, and lost even the common sense that leaving one's lord was a great sin. Navakodiar, being deeply devout, prayed with firm faith that God would remove the foolish woman's ignorance. Through his special rites her ignorance was destroyed. She recognized the new religion as a fraud, wept, and fell at her husband's feet. Navakodiar taught, 'What benefit comes from worshipping my feet? Worship the golden lotus feet of the Lord, who will remove your sin and grant grace.' Now Maruthavalli shines with a new radiance. Tell it this way and more deliciously. Her change is a raft found when the boat has broken; use it carefully."

Maruthavalli's return became a festival in orthodox circles. Everyone spoke of it and supplied a different explanation. She said nothing, though her face clearly revealed an inward resolution. Navakodiar moved majestically around town.

He fixed a day for special worship at the Veludaiyan temple. Maruthavalli would visit for darshan and then explain to the assembled devotees why she abandoned the new faith and proclaim the greatness of the old. News reached the king. The entire region stirred; people said Dhanappan might become royal preceptor.

Many blessed the holy woman returning to orthodoxy. Rich people sent offerings through Maruthavalli for the special worship. She too asked Navakodiar for an offering: costly coral necklaces. Delighted that danger would pass and status rise, he heaped them before her. Wearing them, she looked at herself and laughed.

"What madness is this, Marutham? Such obsession with coral? There are diamonds and other costly necklaces."

"I have an uncontrollable love of coral," she replied.

Firecrackers thundered. Announcements went out for the next day's worship. Vedas were chanted in Maruthavalli's mansion and bhajan groups paraded through the streets. Dhanappan happily gave Vennila a new diamond necklace.

At midnight the poet lay turning anxiously. A consumptive patient's cough sounded like a firecracker. Maruthavalli ran in gasping and dropped the bundle from her head.

"Father! Father!"

"Who is it? My daughter? Marutham?"

"Yes, Father, it is I."

"Daughter, why at midnight?"

"This is my final night, Father. At your feet are coral necklaces - enough to prepare all the coral bhasma required. He gave me all of them. Tomorrow is the festival at which I must betray you. As long as I live, he remains in danger. Prepare the medicine and save these people. This is all I could do. I can serve no further, Father. This is the extent of my service to the Buddhist path. Good coral, Father, fine and costly coral, as the physician asked... coral bhasma..."

The poet wailed aloud. Those nearby rose crying. They saw Maruthavalli's corpse.

In the end, poison had triumphed.

7 July 1946

---NOTES---
“பவழபஸ்பம்” is retained as “coral bhasma,” a medicinal preparation made by calcining coral. The title joins the refugees’ need for medicine to Maruthavalli’s conversion of luxury jewellery into relief.

The story uses “old/ancient faith” and “new path” as politically charged narrative labels. The latter is Buddhism; the former is the orthodox Vedic/Sanatana order aligned with royal and priestly authority.

The final sentence, “விஷம் வென்று விட்டது,” literally says that poison won. It encompasses Maruthavalli’s suicide, the poison of repression and manipulation, and the bitter cost of obtaining the medicine.

Twenty-one readable scan pages were absent from the earlier OCR because their PNG files marked white as transparent. All three parts were regenerated and merged before translation; damaged readings remain verbatim in the Tamil source and are logged separately.

